eport to indicate parenthetic  statements this was something I'm more  or less lifted directly from say Frank  Hank green and  Peter because it is a very effective  form of visual punctuation with  different areas of the set and the frame  used to indicate different types of  statements it also really helps to break  up long complex ideas into smaller more  manageable bites this can serve to  properly differentiate a joke from a  serious statement by moving either very  far from or very close to the camera or  going back to my own example it can  create an aside by literally moving  aside this all has a direct narrative  value in helping better communicate  meaning which is great when you're  trying to get across a complex idea or  unambiguously indicate that something is  a joke now many of these techniques like  dramatically changing framing for  emphasis can be replicated with  conventional cutting this gag wood brach  Mansley where I keep zooming closer and  closer for comedic effect has the same  root idea which is replicating a sense  of unease and invasion of personal space  that would come along with physically  moving myself closer to the camera  however there's a specific device that  can only be achieved by using jump cuts  and that is the use of the set as a  stage when a performer opts to set their  camera and leave the framing unchanged  the resulting video takes on more of the  characteristics of theatre where  specific locations within the stage take  on meanings that aren't properly  replicated purely by position within the  frame while a close-up works to create a  psychological sense of closeness that  effect is amplified if it's cast against  a fixed canvas that more directly  communicates the speaker moving  physically closer to the camera and by  proxy the viewer all of the meanings of  depths are made more potent by giving  more meaning to near and far likewise  moving to the left to indicate an aside  is more potent the more meaning left has  if less is a physical characteristic of  the space rather than simply an  implication created by the movement of  the camera to put another way stage left  is more meaningful than frame 'lest  what this translates into is  the jump-cut style allows for a degree  of intimacy that other editing styles  can't quite replicate by virtue of how  it creates a stage rather than just a  frame this ability to set a stage and in  that stage create a replica of the  intimacy of theater is I think a unique  strength of the style I wanted to  emphasize this first because I do think  it's important to defend the aesthetic  value of the jump-cut rather than just  saying well it solves a bunch of  pragmatic problems speaking of that  jump-cut solves so many pragmatic  problems it's not even funny for the  current iteration of the channel I use  close-ups to cover edits and to create  emphasis  I am almost always on screen because  lip-reading is a fundamental pedagogical  tool many people can absorb information  better or faster when they are watching  a face say it rather than just hearing  it and it minimizes my conflicts with  copyright robots by minimizing how much  copyright content I need to use on  screen  however this in turn means that  everything needs to be recorded on  camera and because I have a personal  desire to make my image quality as good  as I can as a small way of standing out  from the crowd I don't want to make  close-ups by just zooming in like look  at this look at the drop in clarity  that's better this all means that every  video is recorded twice at minimum all  the way through in order to get all the  footage that's going to be needed to  weave everything together which straight  up doubles my record time now that's me  that's my process is it worth it maybe  there is however no question that using  jump cuts instead dramatically  simplifies the process and that's for me  in an environment where I have the  control where I can record things  multiple times and I can change the  camera configuration the jump cut simply  gets more valuable the less control the  creator has or the more chaotic their  environment for people who use real  environments again that's real with a  capital R as opposed to a poised  environment like a studio or set there  simply isn't another practical solution  for the vast majority of creators the  number of youtubers running around with  a small crew who could even  Ford to have two cameras running as they  run around to make their stuff is at  most a few dozen and even then it  wouldn't necessarily serve their  aesthetic needs at the end of the day  single camera confessional style is  cheap and fast and that's not a bad  thing because it still takes skill and  thought to do cheap and fast well it's  easier to use the space as a stage or to  use your environment of the canvas than  it is to fuss around with multiple  camera setups and all of the extra gear  and hassle that goes along with it but  it still takes forethought and  creativity to use those mechanisms well  to find a good canvas to know how and  when to use your stage to punctuate your  point but that skill and forethought is  all something that you can learn as  someone running around on their own with  a camera  you don't need eight other people on  your team to figure out how to do it and  how to do it well so with all that I  think that no matter how many people  post in the comments about the YouTube  style and the jump cut and how much they  hate it the jump cut is here to stay  [Music]
 

